1
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:17,880
*suspensefully pulsing music*

2
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:28,600
*groaning*

3
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,040
*music continues*

4
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:50,040
*gagging*

5
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,680
*spitting, weary groans*

6
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:18,000
*water running*

7
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:22,280
*heavy breathing*

8
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:28,520
*footsteps approaching*

9
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:29,680
Herr Böhm.

10
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:46,920
Good morning.

11
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:49,960
Must have dozed off.

12
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Well then.

13
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:02,120
See you later.

14
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:03,840
Mhm.

15
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:20,440
*eerie music*

16
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:47,280
*rhythmic music*

17
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:04,480
*door is unlocked*

18
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:08,240
*gloomy music*

19
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,240
*weary breathing*

20
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:19,440
*Heymann groans*
- (SA Man) Move it, man!

21
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:21,480
*he groans*

22
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:23,360
(SA Man) Heave-ho.

23
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:26,160
*agitated clamor of voices*

24
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:41,280
*prisoners coughing*

25
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:48,880
*ominous music*

26
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:52,280
Morning, gentlemen.

27
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:58,280
Does anyone among you gentlemen
have anything to smoke?

28
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:00,520
*prisoner shakes head*

29
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:01,880
Him over there.

30
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:08,520
A cigarette?

31
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:10,480
One drag.

32
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:13,960
*painful groan*
- Excuse me.

33
00:04:18,079 --> 00:04:19,320
(Heymann) Comrade.

34
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,040
Comrade.

35
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:41,800
*injured man groans*

36
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:54,680
(whispers) Rath?
Is there a doctor in here?

37
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:57,280
Yes.
- (Heymann) Over here.

38
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:00,280
Or would you prefer, Dr. Kornfeld?
- After you, Mandel.

39
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:03,680
Let me take a look.

40
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:06,960
Ah, we'll need to stitch this up.

41
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:10,120
Onto that crate with him.
Lend a hand, please.

42
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:11,480
Come on now.

43
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:13,680
*sprightly piano music*

44
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:55,680
*music continues*

45
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:57,480
*barking*
- (Woman) Stop it, Karl!

46
00:05:58,560 --> 00:05:59,600
Down!

47
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:11,880
*music fades out*

48
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:25,920
(Böhm) You have got to be kidding me.

49
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:28,840
I know this man.

50
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:34,400
He was in Homicide. Gave a statement.

51
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:40,760
A military paybook.

52
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:46,840
Krajewski, Franz, Corporal.

53
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:51,400
Grand Ducal Mecklenburgian
Fusilier Regiment 90.

54
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:57,880
Take photographs of the crime scene.
Face, stab wound, everything.

55
00:06:57,960 --> 00:06:59,520
What kind of statement did he make?

56
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:02,280
I'll explain that to you later.
Take your photos first.

57
00:07:02,360 --> 00:07:04,080
*footsteps recede*

58
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:08,800
*wooden clatter*

59
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:12,920
(Böhm) Herr Gräf.

60
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:17,920
At the risk of you misunderstanding me.

61
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:19,680
Not a word to our colleagues in Homicide

62
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:21,280
about today's corpse.

63
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Why?

64
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:25,520
I want to handle this case in peace,

65
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:27,640
and I don't want you talking about it.

66
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:30,800
That is rather disconcerting, colleague.

67
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:33,280
Yes, in times like these,
many things are disconcerting.

68
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:37,480
I want to prevent Wendt's people

69
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:39,320
from snatching this case from under our noses as well.

70
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:42,720
You must help me.

71
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:45,720
I don't trust the new colleagues in Pathology.

72
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:48,840
If I show up at the autopsy,
they'll run straight to Wendt.

73
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:51,200
I share that impression, yes.

74
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:54,240
With you, they won't suspect a thing.

75
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:57,440
You have to go down there anyway.

76
00:07:58,040 --> 00:07:59,040
True.

77
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:01,920
Good.
- Thank you.

78
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,640
*lively piano music resumes*

79
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:08,760
*background conversation*

80
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:10,840
*shop bell chimes*

81
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:17,960
Good.

82
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:22,760
I am the youngest
of the six Luxemburg siblings,

83
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:26,440
Anna, Maxymilian,
Mikolaj, Jozef, Rozalia.

84
00:08:26,520 --> 00:08:28,920
They were born in Zamość...

85
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,480
(murmurs) I was born in Warsaw.

86
00:08:31,640 --> 00:08:33,200
The maternal...
*she sighs*

87
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:34,799
Oh God, oh God.

88
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:40,600
Well then,
break a leg, Elisabeth.

89
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:42,799
Ah, it'll turn out fine.

90
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:46,400
*phone rings*

91
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:48,720
*festive piano music*

92
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:50,920
*silverware clatters*

93
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:56,320
*quiet conversations*

94
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:57,800
*music fades out*

95
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:00,440
(Helfers) Frau Luxemburg?

96
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:04,960
What are you doing here?

97
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:07,240
How do you know who my appointment is with?

98
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:10,720
Excuse me? I have an appointment
with Frau Luxemburg.

99
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,240
With Clara Luxemburg?

100
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:14,160
That is correct.

101
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:18,920
Please, do have a seat, ladies.

102
00:09:25,480 --> 00:09:28,000
Thank you for being able to make it.

103
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:32,320
Kawa Polska
with sugar, salt, and cinnamon.

104
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:34,920
(Waiter) I'm sorry, we don't serve that.
- A mocha, then.

105
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:37,320
The same for me.
- Gladly.

106
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:39,120
Merci.

107
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:44,880
I owe you an explanation.

108
00:09:45,760 --> 00:09:48,520
You are asking yourselves: Who is this woman?

109
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:50,400
Why have we never heard of her?

110
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:52,400
Indeed.
- Why is she entitled

111
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:54,720
to convey the innermost wish
of her late sister to us?

112
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:55,800
to us?

113
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:56,960
Yes.

114
00:09:57,440 --> 00:09:58,880
*footsteps approaching*

115
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:00,440
*she sighs*

116
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:04,120
(Behnke) Mhm.

117
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:06,600
Thank you.
- You're welcome.

118
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:12,360
Well, I am the youngest
of the six Luxemburg siblings.

119
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:15,240
Anna, Maxymilian,
Mikolaj, Jozef, Rozalia.

120
00:10:15,320 --> 00:10:17,160
They were all born in Zamość,

121
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:19,280
I, however, was born in Warsaw.

122
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:22,280
After our relocation,
after our late father

123
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:25,400
had to endure his unmerited bankruptcy.

124
00:10:25,560 --> 00:10:28,200
The maternal Löwenstein side

125
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:31,040
went to Berlin early on
with Uncle Salomo,

126
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:35,040
long before Mikolaj married his Anni
in Wilmersdorf.

127
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:37,120
They still live there today.

128
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:39,000
As the baby of the family, my parents were old;

129
00:10:39,080 --> 00:10:41,000
they passed away
when I was in my fourth year.

130
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:43,520
And so I was fostered into Anni's family,

131
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:46,280
took her surname,
Kazimirowska,

132
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:48,840
and thus the visible kinship
with Rozalia vanished.

133
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,000
vanished.

134
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:52,600
You spoke of your sister's innermost wish.

135
00:10:55,760 --> 00:10:57,200
Correct.
- Mhm.

136
00:10:57,280 --> 00:10:59,080
It concerns a note

137
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:01,600
she made for her speech
in the run-up to the founding congress.

138
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:03,160
for her speech.

139
00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:04,680
Of the KPD?

140
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:07,040
That is correct, yes.

141
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:10,840
Um, she delivered her keynote address
on the party program

142
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:12,280
and the political situation.

143
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:16,200
That was agenda item 3,
after Paul Levi, before Hugo Eberlein.

144
00:11:18,280 --> 00:11:20,920
You know that?
- Moscow cadre school.

145
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:23,680
Well, in any case.

146
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:27,960
Up to that point, she was still
a member of your party, the SPD.

147
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:30,440
Leaving was not easy for her,

148
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:32,560
as she confided in our brother.

149
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:35,920
Yet she could not accept what she saw
as bourgeois inclinations...

150
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:37,440
Well, Frau Luxemburg,

151
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:40,280
that is well known to everyone
at this table.

152
00:11:40,720 --> 00:11:41,960
Naturally.

153
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:43,040
The note.

154
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:45,320
The wish.

155
00:11:45,480 --> 00:11:47,720
(Helfers) What did your sister have in mind?

156
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:50,840
The Popular Front.

157
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:52,320
What?
- Pardon?

158
00:11:53,440 --> 00:11:56,560
An alliance of both parties
against the reaction.

159
00:11:57,760 --> 00:12:01,000
Why didn't she mention this proposal
in her speech?

160
00:12:01,080 --> 00:12:02,160
In her speech.
- Yes.

161
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:03,240
Yes.

162
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:06,400
Pressure.

163
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:09,480
Pressure?
- Yes, pressure.

164
00:12:10,120 --> 00:12:11,560
You mentioned it yourself.

165
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:14,400
Besides my sister, Paul Levi was there,

166
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:16,160
Hugo Eberlein, Karl Liebknecht,

167
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:18,560
Hermann Duncker,
Paul Lange, Wilhelm Pieck,

168
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:21,720
Ernst Meyer, and so forth.
What do you notice?

169
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:24,320
No women.

170
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:28,800
The gentlemen did not want a joint,

171
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:32,440
fraternal alliance
of the leftist workers' parties.

172
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:36,440
Yet that was precisely
Rosa Luxemburg's innermost wish.

173
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:39,120
That is why I am meeting with you here today.

174
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:44,560
You are the left wing of fascism.

175
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:46,880
Your party's aim is the dissolution...

176
00:12:46,960 --> 00:12:48,600
Social Democracy is...

177
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:50,120
*clamor of voices*

178
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:53,440
One moment!

179
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:56,000
May I ask you a question?

180
00:12:57,800 --> 00:12:59,640
Of course.
- By all means.

181
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:04,600
So even in light of this, shall we say,

182
00:13:04,760 --> 00:13:09,200
emergency for our democracy,
you rule out a temporary alliance

183
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:11,080
for the upcoming election?

184
00:13:11,160 --> 00:13:12,160
No.

185
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:14,400
Oh really?

186
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:18,240
Upon unconditional submission
of your party to our KPD,

187
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:21,280
we could very well envision joining forces.

188
00:13:22,320 --> 00:13:23,320
Hold on.

189
00:13:25,080 --> 00:13:28,120
Discussion is not productive at this point.

190
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:32,240
I give you 24 hours to consider it.

191
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:35,800
I am in close contact
with influential figures

192
00:13:35,960 --> 00:13:39,120
from the press and the judiciary
who demand this unification.

193
00:13:40,040 --> 00:13:42,640
If you care at all
about my sister's life's work,

194
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:45,160
or about the future of Germany,

195
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:46,960
we have no time to lose.

196
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:49,560
Tomorrow, 10 AM,

197
00:13:49,640 --> 00:13:52,240
Bayreuther Straße 25,
I shall await you.

198
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:54,120
Good day, ladies.

199
00:13:56,200 --> 00:13:58,040
*soulful music*

200
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:03,520
*she sighs*

201
00:14:11,160 --> 00:14:12,960
(Wendt) That is Inspector Rath, yes.

202
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:15,120
Where did this photograph come from?

203
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:16,720
Paris.

204
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:20,000
He was arrested there,
after a few detours.

205
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:23,720
Paris? I fear I have no jurisdiction there.
Not yet.

206
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:27,680
An internal party matter, you said?

207
00:14:28,760 --> 00:14:30,400
This concerns high treason.

208
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:33,800
I made sure he won't be sent
to regular detention.

209
00:14:33,880 --> 00:14:37,320
On the contrary.
He must disappear, forever.

210
00:14:38,840 --> 00:14:41,240
I will do everything within my power.

211
00:14:41,320 --> 00:14:44,880
He's been a thorn in my side for a long time,
that Inspector.

212
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:48,760
What exactly drove him to Paris?

213
00:14:51,440 --> 00:14:54,280
First it was an internal party matter.

214
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:56,400
Now it is a top secret state affair.

215
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:00,160
He helped an enemy of the movement escape.

216
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:02,480
I am not permitted to disclose more,
even to you.

217
00:15:03,200 --> 00:15:04,600
I am sorry.

218
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:09,360
*Wendt clears his throat*

219
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:12,040
*tense music*

220
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:27,160
Chief Inspector.

221
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:28,800
Government Councillor.

222
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:32,160
What can you tell me about the whereabouts
of Inspector Rath?

223
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:39,360
(Gennat) Following a botched arrest,
Inspector Rath failed to report for duty.

224
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:41,280
failed to report for duty.

225
00:15:41,360 --> 00:15:42,640
He has been logged as missing.

226
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:45,160
A nationwide search is underway,
that is all.

227
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:47,840
What was that failed arrest concerning?

228
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,920
A homicide in medical circles.

229
00:15:51,080 --> 00:15:55,200
The receptionist of a certain Dr. Forster
was murdered.

230
00:15:55,280 --> 00:15:57,640
Is that so?
- Inspector Rath attempted

231
00:15:57,800 --> 00:16:01,160
to apprehend this man, who may potentially
also be considered a perpetrator.

232
00:16:01,240 --> 00:16:02,280
to apprehend him.

233
00:16:02,440 --> 00:16:04,080
He did not succeed.

234
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:07,360
And thereafter, he disappeared.

235
00:16:10,280 --> 00:16:12,440
You can call off the search.

236
00:16:12,520 --> 00:16:13,840
(Gennat) And why is that?

237
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:15,800
Because Inspector Rath has been located.

238
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:19,320
Abroad, where he absconded without leave.

239
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:20,840
(Gennat) Where exactly?

240
00:16:20,920 --> 00:16:22,240
(Wendt) Paris.

241
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:24,840
You do not know what took him there?

242
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:28,600
No, I'm afraid not.

243
00:16:30,440 --> 00:16:32,480
High treason is in the air.

244
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:35,800
The Police President has suspended him
from duty,

245
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:37,600
with immediate effect.

246
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:41,720
*suspensefully pulsing music*

247
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:46,840
How is your new contingent
of personnel working out, by the way?

248
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,920
A useful instrument the Police President
has provided us with, isn't it?

249
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:52,120
isn't it?

250
00:16:52,280 --> 00:16:54,320
The largest staff increase in the history

251
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:56,640
of the civil service.

252
00:16:57,160 --> 00:16:59,160
After such a short time in office.

253
00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:08,760
Where is Gereon Rath now?

254
00:17:16,040 --> 00:17:19,160
Miss Ritter,
you should bear the following in mind:

255
00:17:19,240 --> 00:17:22,800
If you know anything about his illegal activities

256
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:25,800
and withhold it from me,
you are committing a crime.

257
00:17:27,319 --> 00:17:29,320
And then it will be me prosecuting you.

258
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:39,160
*traffic noise*

259
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:44,200
*car horns*

260
00:17:47,440 --> 00:17:48,680
Herr Katelbach.

261
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:54,560
Frau Elisabeth, I almost didn't recognize you.

262
00:17:54,720 --> 00:17:57,360
Aha. Yet you still turned your head to look.

263
00:17:57,440 --> 00:18:00,400
Admittedly, yes. How could I not,
with such a feast for the eyes?

264
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:01,960
You look dashing.

265
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:04,080
Where are you coming from?

266
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:07,120
I was at the hairdresser's. And you?

267
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:09,920
Ran out of cigarettes.
I'll be right back.

268
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:11,160
Mhm.

269
00:18:16,280 --> 00:18:18,280
*dark music*

270
00:18:22,440 --> 00:18:26,240
Criminal Police, stay seated.
We are checking your identity.

271
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:29,640
(SA Man) Over there.
- Identification.

272
00:18:29,800 --> 00:18:31,520
Identification.
- Papers.

273
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:33,800
(SA Man) Halt!

274
00:18:33,960 --> 00:18:35,840
There! Up ahead.

275
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:38,280
Halt, stop right there!

276
00:18:39,960 --> 00:18:41,720
Open up!
- Open up!

277
00:18:42,680 --> 00:18:43,880
Open up immediately!

278
00:18:44,440 --> 00:18:45,920
*battle cry*

279
00:18:49,840 --> 00:18:51,520
Name?
- Matern.

280
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:52,960
Max.

281
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:55,000
Camp Central.

282
00:18:55,160 --> 00:18:56,360
Next.

283
00:18:57,160 --> 00:19:01,040
(SA Man) Jonigkeit,
Müller, Schmidt...

284
00:19:04,160 --> 00:19:05,160
Thank you, Heymann.

285
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:10,000
Thank these two luminaries here.

286
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:11,960
Mandel stitched you up.

287
00:19:12,440 --> 00:19:14,840
(Mandel) We took the thread
from your suit.

288
00:19:14,920 --> 00:19:17,080
(Kornfeld) Don't touch it!
*he groans*

289
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:22,360
I think you'd better not stand up yet.

290
00:19:24,200 --> 00:19:25,360
Where are we?

291
00:19:26,560 --> 00:19:30,560
(Heymann) Tempelhof.
SA Barracks. In purgatory.

292
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:35,880
*indistinct shouts*

293
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:39,800
Line up back there.

294
00:19:41,160 --> 00:19:42,440
Henning?

295
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:44,440
Czerwinski?

296
00:19:45,560 --> 00:19:46,720
Henning!

297
00:19:47,600 --> 00:19:50,720
Czerwinski! Henning!
- They won't hear you.

298
00:19:52,240 --> 00:19:53,720
How do we get out of here?

299
00:19:55,600 --> 00:19:58,880
I'm afraid the only way out
is the same way we came in.

300
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:00,480
In a truck.

301
00:20:01,080 --> 00:20:03,040
(SA Man) Camp North!
Camp North?

302
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:07,360
(Brock) They're clearing out whole cells
and hauling them to Oranienburg.

303
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:10,480
(Kornfeld) Mhm.
- In regular rotation.

304
00:20:10,640 --> 00:20:13,480
(Kornfeld) Cell one,
cell two, and so on.

305
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:15,840
How many men guard a transport like that?

306
00:20:15,920 --> 00:20:17,880
(Brock)
One or two in the cab up front.

307
00:20:18,040 --> 00:20:20,880
And two Brownshirts in back
with the prisoners.

308
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:24,000
Armed.

309
00:20:24,680 --> 00:20:26,880
Yes.
Then we must...

310
00:20:29,240 --> 00:20:30,440
During the drive.

311
00:20:33,160 --> 00:20:34,480
*door opens*

312
00:20:34,560 --> 00:20:36,800
(SA Man) Right, in here now.

313
00:20:36,960 --> 00:20:38,680
*door is locked*

314
00:20:38,840 --> 00:20:41,240
Matern? Max?
- Brock?

315
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:43,720
*SA men laughing in the distance*

316
00:20:45,880 --> 00:20:47,400
(Brock) Where did they pick you up?

317
00:20:47,880 --> 00:20:50,760
(Matern) Party headquarters.
- Transport to Camp North.

318
00:20:50,920 --> 00:20:52,760
*vehicle hums*

319
00:20:57,520 --> 00:21:00,080
*indistinct conversation*

320
00:21:13,520 --> 00:21:15,680
*background chatter,
phone ringing*

321
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:18,800
Any new developments, Chief Inspector?

322
00:21:19,520 --> 00:21:20,680
What business is that of yours?

323
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:35,320
Right, Krajewski, Franz.

324
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:40,720
'Discharged from military service.
Pasewalk Hospital. Forster.'

325
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:42,400
The connection.

326
00:21:49,040 --> 00:21:50,720
*phone rings*

327
00:21:55,200 --> 00:21:57,280
Yes?
Ritter, Homicide.

328
00:21:57,440 --> 00:21:59,560
*sinister music*

329
00:22:02,960 --> 00:22:04,000
What do you want?

330
00:22:04,560 --> 00:22:07,400
Since our last conversation,
further questions have arisen.

331
00:22:07,480 --> 00:22:09,760
I need to speak with you.
In 15 minutes.

332
00:22:09,840 --> 00:22:12,040
Fine.
I have something to tell you too.

333
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:32,360
That we are working together

334
00:22:32,440 --> 00:22:34,520
doesn't seem to be sinking in, does it?

335
00:22:36,720 --> 00:22:40,720
I still have to get used to it, apparently.

336
00:22:47,640 --> 00:22:49,920
Well then, enjoy your lunch.

337
00:22:57,920 --> 00:22:59,960
*bells tolling*

338
00:23:03,840 --> 00:23:05,240
*she sighs*

339
00:23:05,320 --> 00:23:07,440
*footsteps approaching*

340
00:23:12,720 --> 00:23:15,240
Here I am. What did you want?

341
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:16,320
You first.

342
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:19,600
Let's walk a few steps.

343
00:23:22,600 --> 00:23:24,560
There is another murder victim.

344
00:23:25,120 --> 00:23:28,320
Franz Krajewski, the man
who was in my office yesterday.

345
00:23:28,400 --> 00:23:31,640
I hope to learn details from Pathology shortly.

346
00:23:31,720 --> 00:23:33,680
Yet by all appearances, Krajewski was stabbed

347
00:23:33,760 --> 00:23:36,040
in the same manner as Strelow.

348
00:23:36,760 --> 00:23:39,080
And both were in Pasewalk
at the end of the war.

349
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:41,040
I gathered that from his paybook.

350
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:42,360
What?
Yes.

351
00:23:42,520 --> 00:23:43,960
Like Wosniak and Forster.

352
00:23:44,840 --> 00:23:46,080
And Hitler.

353
00:23:47,160 --> 00:23:50,320
He wants to eradicate all traces
leading back to that hospital.

354
00:23:50,400 --> 00:23:51,480
Files, photos.

355
00:23:51,640 --> 00:23:54,000
He's having them liquidated,
one by one.

356
00:23:54,080 --> 00:23:55,920
Everyone who saw him there.

357
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,680
So that no one can contradict his version
of history.

358
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:02,840
We must assemble everything
before we take this to Gennat.

359
00:24:02,920 --> 00:24:04,360
We're talking about the Reich Chancellor.

360
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:07,960
He won't handle us with kid gloves either.

361
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:11,600
How do you explain the murders
of the receptionist and the patrolman?

362
00:24:11,680 --> 00:24:14,640
Wrong place, wrong time, I presume.

363
00:24:14,720 --> 00:24:16,720
Hitler wants witnesses eliminated,
Forster above all.

364
00:24:20,400 --> 00:24:22,160
Why did you want to speak with me?

365
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:25,080
*she clears her throat*

366
00:24:25,680 --> 00:24:28,520
The liquor.
The cognac, to be precise.

367
00:24:30,200 --> 00:24:31,280
Which cognac?

368
00:24:31,360 --> 00:24:33,680
You know which cognac I mean.

369
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:36,040
A very special vintage from France.

370
00:24:36,720 --> 00:24:39,560
And this cognac?
You intended to drink it?

371
00:24:39,640 --> 00:24:41,680
No,
but I would like it back.

372
00:24:43,880 --> 00:24:46,680
Does it belong to you,
or to colleague Rath?

373
00:24:51,560 --> 00:24:53,680
I'm listening.
*she sighs*

374
00:24:57,600 --> 00:24:59,000
To colleague Rath.

375
00:24:59,160 --> 00:25:01,360
Aha, just as I thought.

376
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:05,160
Have you ever considered that colleague Rath

377
00:25:05,240 --> 00:25:07,240
was set on Forster?

378
00:25:07,880 --> 00:25:09,000
By whom?

379
00:25:09,080 --> 00:25:11,200
I was thinking of the National Socialists.

380
00:25:11,280 --> 00:25:13,560
Wouldn't be the first time.
Out of the question.

381
00:25:14,200 --> 00:25:15,640
You are sure of that?

382
00:25:20,160 --> 00:25:21,520
You see?

383
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:38,200
Right.

384
00:25:39,240 --> 00:25:41,520
Now we wait for the autopsy results.

385
00:25:43,240 --> 00:25:46,400
And then we plan our next move, agreed?

386
00:25:47,480 --> 00:25:49,560
*solemn brass music*

387
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:57,000
*indistinct clamor*

388
00:25:57,440 --> 00:25:58,760
What's going on over there?

389
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:01,000
SPD rally against Hitler.

390
00:26:01,880 --> 00:26:03,960
We shouldn't return to the Burg together.

391
00:26:04,040 --> 00:26:05,920
Best call it a day.

392
00:26:08,360 --> 00:26:09,400
Hm.

393
00:26:19,680 --> 00:26:21,840
*music grows louder*

394
00:26:21,920 --> 00:26:24,160
*scattered calls*

395
00:26:28,120 --> 00:26:30,840
*clamor of voices swells*

396
00:26:51,680 --> 00:26:54,040
*crowd chants*

397
00:26:57,160 --> 00:26:58,320
How many are there?

398
00:26:58,480 --> 00:27:01,800
Police estimate 200,000.

399
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:03,960
Had the Communists marched too,

400
00:27:04,040 --> 00:27:07,040
it could have been half a million
in Berlin alone.

401
00:27:07,120 --> 00:27:10,320
A camel will pass through the eye of a needle
before a Communist

402
00:27:10,400 --> 00:27:13,800
shows up at a Social Democratic rally.

403
00:27:16,840 --> 00:27:19,200
Except for the Doctor and her hard core.

404
00:27:19,360 --> 00:27:21,400
(Crowd) Social Democrats, Berlin!

405
00:27:21,880 --> 00:27:24,200
Berlin, Berlin stays red!

406
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:26,960
We are the Social Democrats!

407
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:28,160
Something, at least.

408
00:27:28,320 --> 00:27:32,080
(Speaker) The Chairman of the
Social Democratic Party speaks:

409
00:27:32,160 --> 00:27:33,200
Otto Wels.

410
00:27:33,360 --> 00:27:34,840
*applause*

411
00:27:34,920 --> 00:27:36,760
*crowd continues chanting*

412
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:44,120
(Crowd) Bravo!

413
00:27:48,600 --> 00:27:49,680
Comrades!

414
00:27:50,680 --> 00:27:51,960
Friends!

415
00:27:52,400 --> 00:27:53,520
Berliners!

416
00:27:53,600 --> 00:27:55,520
*roaring applause*

417
00:27:58,840 --> 00:28:00,880
*dynamic music*

418
00:28:00,960 --> 00:28:03,600
*speech continues in background*

419
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:16,240
*music turns more mysterious*

420
00:28:23,320 --> 00:28:25,360
*echoing applause*

421
00:28:52,440 --> 00:28:54,760
*music turns more suspenseful*

422
00:29:01,040 --> 00:29:02,640
*clatter*

423
00:29:14,120 --> 00:29:15,560
*roaring applause*

424
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:22,480
*music stops abruptly*

425
00:29:26,240 --> 00:29:27,560
(Wels) No!

426
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:34,080
Today a single idea unites us all.

427
00:29:34,920 --> 00:29:36,960
Whether man or woman,

428
00:29:37,120 --> 00:29:39,480
whether radical or moderate,

429
00:29:39,640 --> 00:29:42,080
we shall prevent

430
00:29:42,240 --> 00:29:45,440
the barbarians from ruling over us!

431
00:29:45,600 --> 00:29:47,600
*echoing applause*

432
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:01,440
Jacky!
Well, look at that,

433
00:30:01,600 --> 00:30:03,160
Lotte is a Socialist.

434
00:30:03,320 --> 00:30:04,880
(Berlin dialect) I am not.

435
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:06,200
What are you, then?

436
00:30:07,080 --> 00:30:09,600
One of us. And you?
Same here.

437
00:30:10,480 --> 00:30:13,320
Just a bit redder
than most folks here.

438
00:30:13,920 --> 00:30:15,400
Had no idea.
Come on.

439
00:30:20,280 --> 00:30:21,400
Comrades.

440
00:30:22,680 --> 00:30:25,280
Lotte is one of us.

441
00:30:26,960 --> 00:30:28,760
(Comrades) Solidarity.

442
00:30:28,840 --> 00:30:31,360
Solidarity.
We know each other from the Koppeneck.

443
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:32,880
Hm.

444
00:30:33,320 --> 00:30:34,320
Here.

445
00:30:34,920 --> 00:30:39,120
(Wels) ...the free press,
the right of every human being.

446
00:30:41,120 --> 00:30:43,600
A good vantage point for the fire.

447
00:30:44,600 --> 00:30:50,600
(Wels) Nothing and no one
will stop us!

448
00:30:51,040 --> 00:30:52,720
*thundering applause*

449
00:30:52,880 --> 00:30:56,280
*"Wann wir schreiten Seit' an Seit'"
by Michael Englert*

450
00:30:56,440 --> 00:30:58,160
*triumphant music*

451
00:30:58,240 --> 00:31:01,800
# When we march side by side #

452
00:31:01,960 --> 00:31:06,040
# And the old songs we sing #

453
00:31:06,200 --> 00:31:09,840
# And the forest arches ring #

454
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:14,920
# We feel our cause must win: #

455
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:20,800
# With us marches the new era #

456
00:31:21,240 --> 00:31:27,800
# With us marches the new era! #

457
00:31:27,960 --> 00:31:29,520
*singing fades out*

458
00:31:31,800 --> 00:31:32,920
Pardon me?

459
00:31:34,880 --> 00:31:37,800
How can it be that we fail
to prevent such a mass gathering?

460
00:31:37,960 --> 00:31:38,960
to prevent it?

461
00:31:39,040 --> 00:31:40,960
The SPD knows full well
what protects them.

462
00:31:41,680 --> 00:31:42,920
The Weimar Constitution.

463
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:45,800
Right, and tomorrow the KPD comes along

464
00:31:45,880 --> 00:31:48,600
and mobilizes half a million,
is that it?

465
00:31:51,080 --> 00:31:53,960
Why aren't you managing
to intern more enemies?

466
00:31:54,040 --> 00:31:56,240
Because laws exist.

467
00:31:57,600 --> 00:32:00,760
Yet to deliver a truly crushing blow,

468
00:32:00,840 --> 00:32:02,760
we require an altered legal framework.

469
00:32:02,840 --> 00:32:05,600
And that must be substantiated.

470
00:32:05,760 --> 00:32:08,400
A national state of emergency.

471
00:32:09,480 --> 00:32:11,440
That is what we need.

472
00:32:13,640 --> 00:32:14,760
You are right.

473
00:32:16,480 --> 00:32:19,040
We must suspend the constitution.

474
00:32:20,360 --> 00:32:24,120
Precisely.
The question is how we bring that about.

475
00:32:25,400 --> 00:32:27,520
*suspenseful music*

476
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:34,560
Didn't work.

477
00:32:36,360 --> 00:32:39,520
We have to get inside,
set the blaze from within.

478
00:32:41,480 --> 00:32:43,000
*music turns more sinister*

479
00:32:48,280 --> 00:32:49,320
Herr Böhm!

480
00:32:50,320 --> 00:32:53,640
Herr Böhm.
I've just come from Pathology.

481
00:32:53,720 --> 00:32:55,000
And?
- Too late.

482
00:32:55,080 --> 00:32:56,800
I was told the case was closed.

483
00:32:56,880 --> 00:33:01,080
The body of Franz Krajewski
has already been released.

484
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:03,000
What?
- Yes.

485
00:33:03,080 --> 00:33:06,240
For cremation? The body
was taken to the crematorium?

486
00:33:06,320 --> 00:33:10,160
When there are no relatives,
a body is cremated.

487
00:33:10,240 --> 00:33:12,440
To destroy all evidence, is that it?

488
00:33:13,160 --> 00:33:14,760
Do they take us for fools?

489
00:33:14,840 --> 00:33:16,880
Or are they simply brazen now?

490
00:33:16,960 --> 00:33:18,680
They haven't seen the last of me.

491
00:33:25,080 --> 00:33:27,160
*background chatter,
phone ringing*

492
00:33:27,320 --> 00:33:29,440
*eerie music*

493
00:33:38,400 --> 00:33:39,600
*murmuring*

494
00:34:03,800 --> 00:34:05,160
We had no time.

495
00:34:05,720 --> 00:34:07,080
Nor a key.

496
00:34:08,080 --> 00:34:11,320
Had to secure files
you were withholding from us.

497
00:34:14,719 --> 00:34:17,560
Government Councillor Wendt
has a few questions for you.

498
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:21,000
Now.

499
00:34:23,760 --> 00:34:25,679
No, I have no time right now.

500
00:34:25,760 --> 00:34:27,400
(Sebald) Oh yes, you do.

501
00:34:27,840 --> 00:34:28,960
(Döbrich) Move it!

502
00:34:30,679 --> 00:34:33,280
You certainly do.
- (Sebald) After you.

503
00:34:33,440 --> 00:34:35,199
*music continues*

504
00:34:39,760 --> 00:34:41,239
*Böhm huffs*

505
00:34:43,159 --> 00:34:44,280
Move it now!

506
00:34:56,120 --> 00:34:57,480
*door slams shut*

507
00:35:04,200 --> 00:35:07,120
*music gradually intensifies*

508
00:35:17,240 --> 00:35:18,680
*he sighs*

509
00:35:45,760 --> 00:35:47,680
*music turns restless*

510
00:36:07,080 --> 00:36:09,120
*suspensefully pulsing music*

511
00:36:11,760 --> 00:36:14,120
Herr Böhm, you may see him now.

512
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:33,120
*traffic sounds,
indistinct dialogue*

513
00:36:37,600 --> 00:36:39,680
*music swells somberly*

514
00:36:47,480 --> 00:36:49,880
*cheerful background conversation*

515
00:36:53,600 --> 00:36:55,920
*music fades slowly*

516
00:37:04,680 --> 00:37:07,400
So do you intend to visit us
more often now, Herr Litten?

517
00:37:08,080 --> 00:37:11,360
The SA is on his doorstep.
They want to intimidate him.

518
00:37:11,440 --> 00:37:14,320
And his clients along with him.
- I see.

519
00:37:14,480 --> 00:37:17,360
For the time being, I could also
sleep on the sofa there...

520
00:37:18,560 --> 00:37:20,920
Yes, of course, Herr Litten.

521
00:37:24,440 --> 00:37:27,760
Yet tomorrow morning I expect
two ladies for coffee.

522
00:37:27,840 --> 00:37:29,160
I will need the room then.

523
00:37:29,880 --> 00:37:30,960
Naturally.

524
00:37:33,840 --> 00:37:36,280
Female visitors?
What is she on about?

525
00:37:36,360 --> 00:37:38,120
What ladies are coming?

526
00:37:38,280 --> 00:37:40,160
She never has lady visitors.

527
00:37:42,680 --> 00:37:44,760
Another coffee after all?
No.

528
00:37:46,440 --> 00:37:47,840
Will we see each other later, though?

529
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:50,320
*distant background chatter*

530
00:38:02,600 --> 00:38:03,720
Not for now.

531
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:05,880
Too late.

532
00:38:07,360 --> 00:38:08,680
*door opens*

533
00:38:09,960 --> 00:38:12,040
*melancholy music*

534
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:15,920
*she sighs*

535
00:38:23,240 --> 00:38:25,160
*music continues*

536
00:38:42,040 --> 00:38:43,800
*music breaks off*

537
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:47,480
*she huffs*
*music resumes*

538
00:38:50,080 --> 00:38:51,920
*music fades out*

539
00:38:52,000 --> 00:38:53,720
(Heymann) Did you serve?

540
00:38:54,240 --> 00:38:56,560
Third East Prussian
Grenadier Regiment.

541
00:38:58,080 --> 00:38:59,600
We can use you.

542
00:39:03,120 --> 00:39:04,440
Matern, Max.

543
00:39:05,600 --> 00:39:08,720
First Lieutenant Heymann,
Second Jäger Regiment.

544
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:11,880
Let's feel out who else we have here.

545
00:39:12,760 --> 00:39:14,240
Who served in the war?

546
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:16,880
(several) Here.
- Then allow me

547
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:19,680
to organize our joint escape.

548
00:39:19,760 --> 00:39:22,600
Any objections?
- (all shake heads)

549
00:39:23,680 --> 00:39:24,680
Good.

550
00:39:25,360 --> 00:39:28,560
Most of you are academics, correct?

551
00:39:28,720 --> 00:39:30,040
(scattered) Yes.

552
00:39:30,120 --> 00:39:31,720
(Brock) I'm a craftsman.

553
00:39:32,840 --> 00:39:34,640
*ominous music*

554
00:39:44,080 --> 00:39:45,080
A blacksmith?

555
00:39:46,040 --> 00:39:47,040
A blacksmith.

556
00:39:47,760 --> 00:39:49,280
*footsteps approaching*

557
00:39:52,440 --> 00:39:54,320
(SA Man) Identification check.

558
00:39:55,640 --> 00:39:59,760
(van der Lubbe) The Reichstag?
- (Albrecht) Mhm. Very heavily guarded.

559
00:39:59,840 --> 00:40:02,840
(van der Lubbe) We can't get in there
unless you climb.

560
00:40:07,200 --> 00:40:08,840
Maybe there's another way.

561
00:40:11,840 --> 00:40:14,000
This is the man I told you about.

562
00:40:14,680 --> 00:40:17,160
Poor eyesight,
speaks poor German.

563
00:40:21,400 --> 00:40:22,640
Come along with me, then.

564
00:40:31,400 --> 00:40:34,520
(Brock) If I counted correctly,
we're up tomorrow.

565
00:40:34,680 --> 00:40:36,480
*suspenseful music*

566
00:40:38,840 --> 00:40:40,560
Strike fast and strike short.

567
00:40:46,920 --> 00:40:48,320
Crucial question:

568
00:40:49,640 --> 00:40:51,320
Who has the stomach for it?

569
00:41:05,200 --> 00:41:06,400
I'll do it.

570
00:41:11,120 --> 00:41:13,680
We should take them both down
at the same time.

571
00:41:13,760 --> 00:41:14,760
Understood.

572
00:41:17,080 --> 00:41:18,080
On your signal?

573
00:41:21,200 --> 00:41:24,800
Then we use their weapons
to take control of the driver's cab.

574
00:41:27,400 --> 00:41:29,160
Sounds like a walk in the park.

575
00:41:32,000 --> 00:41:33,400
*he sighs*

576
00:41:38,200 --> 00:41:40,160
*music continues*

577
00:41:50,560 --> 00:41:52,760
*music mounts dramatically*

578
00:41:54,000 --> 00:41:55,000
*

